WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.283 --> 00:00:04.951
- Welcome Gary Vaynerchuk!

00:00:04.951 --> 00:00:07.784
(audience cheers)

00:00:14.836 --> 00:00:16.968
- Thank you.
- Yeah, man.

00:00:16.968 --> 00:00:19.801
(audience cheers)

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:25.599
- Thank you, Philly, sit, let's do this.

00:00:25.599 --> 00:00:27.510
(audience murmurs)

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.750
What up, what up?

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.087
What up?

00:00:31.087 --> 00:00:33.987
(audience cheers)

00:00:33.987 --> 00:00:35.765
Good to see you, Philly.

00:00:35.765 --> 00:00:38.202
First of all, before we get this started,

00:00:38.202 --> 00:00:42.903
the amount of gratitude
that I have for this city

00:00:42.903 --> 00:00:45.522
for beating the fucking
Patriots in the Super Bowl.

00:00:45.522 --> 00:00:47.228
(audience cheers wildly)

00:00:47.228 --> 00:00:48.645
Let's fucking go!

00:00:50.007 --> 00:00:52.181
Thank you for that.

00:00:52.181 --> 00:00:54.348
Man, I hate those fuckers.

00:00:56.674 --> 00:00:59.641
Today's an extremely special day for me

00:00:59.641 --> 00:01:02.516
because today is my mother's birthday.

00:01:02.516 --> 00:01:04.660
(audience cheers)

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.160
And I thought I would
use that as a framework

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.495
of the opening dialogue, giving a sense of

00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:14.039
who's speaking today.

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:17.638
I won't go too much into
mindset and motivation

00:01:17.638 --> 00:01:19.818
and those kind of things
'cause I think we have a lot

00:01:19.818 --> 00:01:21.710
of people covering it,
I'm actually going to go

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:24.488
pretty practical on the back half of this

00:01:24.488 --> 00:01:29.197
about why I think most of you
are massively missing the mark

00:01:29.197 --> 00:01:32.237
and I include myself in that.

00:01:32.237 --> 00:01:35.512
That we're missing the mark
in this enormous gold rush

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:39.362
of Facebook ads and Instagram
ads, LinkedIn, YouTube.

00:01:39.362 --> 00:01:42.304
No matter how much content
you're producing for whatever

00:01:42.304 --> 00:01:44.749
you're trying to achieve,
whether that's to raise money

00:01:44.749 --> 00:01:48.493
for the PTA or sell sneakers
or build your real estate firm,

00:01:48.493 --> 00:01:51.007
whatever you do, the one
thing, for all the people

00:01:51.007 --> 00:01:53.090
here in the front and all those
homies that I love so much

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.210
in the back--
(audience cheers)

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.129
Man, it takes a while for
noise to get the fuck up here.

00:02:00.129 --> 00:02:02.189
Shit is crazy.
(audience laughs)

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:04.478
The one thing I know
for fact that connects

00:02:04.478 --> 00:02:07.293
every person here, that
cameraman right there,

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:11.960
myself, you, all of you, is
that attention is the asset.

00:02:13.586 --> 00:02:15.541
That before you can tell
me how great you are,

00:02:15.541 --> 00:02:18.673
why I should buy your thing,
why I should do the thing

00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:21.064
that you want me to do,
vote for me, buy my thing,

00:02:21.064 --> 00:02:22.642
do this, help that,

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:26.631
that attention is the asset
and I have spent the last

00:02:26.631 --> 00:02:30.673
20 years of my business
career becoming a practitioner

00:02:30.673 --> 00:02:33.839
in what I call under-priced attention.

00:02:33.839 --> 00:02:36.784
Whether it's email, or
websites, or Google AdWords,

00:02:36.784 --> 00:02:39.707
or YouTube, or Facebook, or Twitter,

00:02:39.707 --> 00:02:43.010
the reason I have the great
luxury of standing up here,

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:45.423
at least for the merit of what
I've been able to accomplish

00:02:45.423 --> 00:02:49.061
in my professional career,
is I've been able to execute

00:02:49.061 --> 00:02:53.915
within real estate that has
been under-priced by the market,

00:02:53.915 --> 00:02:56.760
I've been able to storytell
and execute in there

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.647
and that has led to the
results that I have.

00:02:59.647 --> 00:03:02.465
As I got older, I got
smarter and started investing

00:03:02.465 --> 00:03:06.100
in the platforms that became
the most important real estate

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.249
and those are the
foundations of the things,

00:03:08.249 --> 00:03:12.109
and when I buy the New York
Jets and win by Super Bowls...

00:03:12.109 --> 00:03:14.102
(audience cheers)

00:03:14.102 --> 00:03:16.535
It will, 100%, be on the back

00:03:16.535 --> 00:03:19.684
of the things that I'm
going to talk to you

00:03:19.684 --> 00:03:22.742
on the second half which is
how all of you need to start

00:03:22.742 --> 00:03:26.153
buying and creating on these platforms

00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:28.410
and why so many of you have failed.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.959
How many people here by show
of hands have run Facebook

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:33.391
or Instagram ads and it hasn't worked?

00:03:33.391 --> 00:03:35.964
'Cause I think it's a lot
of people, raise them high.

00:03:35.964 --> 00:03:38.311
There's a very specific reason.

00:03:38.311 --> 00:03:41.575
It's because it's not that
easy, it's not just, you know,

00:03:41.575 --> 00:03:44.401
it's like me saying I'm going
to put a ball in the hoop.

00:03:44.401 --> 00:03:46.490
That's great, but it
doesn't mean I'm going to be

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:47.817
a professional basketball player.

00:03:47.817 --> 00:03:50.443
It takes a skill set,
and I'll get into that,

00:03:50.443 --> 00:03:53.016
and that's the thing, if you
want to ask me what I'm trying

00:03:53.016 --> 00:03:56.955
to accomplish here today,
every person in here is

00:03:56.955 --> 00:04:01.589
under-spending and
under-creating on Facebook,

00:04:01.589 --> 00:04:05.181
LinkedIn, YouTube,
Twitter, podcasting, email,

00:04:05.181 --> 00:04:07.301
everybody, me too.

00:04:07.301 --> 00:04:10.244
And I have 22 full time people,
I'm spending three to four

00:04:10.244 --> 00:04:12.441
million dollars a year
between advertising dollars

00:04:12.441 --> 00:04:15.942
and people and I'm under-doing it.

00:04:15.942 --> 00:04:19.841
So, I really need to deliver
that, that's part two.

00:04:19.841 --> 00:04:23.109
Part one is a little bit
more interesting to me.

00:04:23.109 --> 00:04:28.026
Over the last year, it's become
obvious to me that optimism

00:04:30.019 --> 00:04:31.936
is actually a strategy.

00:04:32.949 --> 00:04:36.099
That I'm fascinated by what I was gifted.

00:04:36.099 --> 00:04:39.623
I was gifted, through my
mom's DNA and by the way

00:04:39.623 --> 00:04:42.717
that she parented me, to
be fundamentally in a place

00:04:42.717 --> 00:04:47.275
where I just am incapable
of seeing the negative.

00:04:47.275 --> 00:04:50.121
I don't really, it's
not that I'm delusional.

00:04:50.121 --> 00:04:53.454
Basically, my life for the last 27 years

00:04:54.912 --> 00:04:56.414
has been pretty basic.

00:04:56.414 --> 00:05:00.486
If I wake up and me and
the seven or eight people

00:05:00.486 --> 00:05:04.153
that I love the most didn't
die, I'm fucking pumped.

00:05:04.153 --> 00:05:06.330
(audience laughs)

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.785
(audience applauds)

00:05:07.785 --> 00:05:11.798
Now I really, really need you
to understand what I'm saying.

00:05:11.798 --> 00:05:14.917
I mean it, it's super basic.

00:05:14.917 --> 00:05:18.721
I have a happy life not
because of my success.

00:05:18.721 --> 00:05:20.766
Seeing my dear high school
friend, Jess, right there,

00:05:20.766 --> 00:05:23.292
one, two, three, in the fifth row...

00:05:23.292 --> 00:05:25.792
It was super fun, I ran into
her in the hotel last night

00:05:25.792 --> 00:05:29.230
and I was there with
her husband and friend

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.911
and it was fun for me,
it was emotional for me

00:05:31.911 --> 00:05:35.278
because she's one of a very,
you know, we only went to

00:05:35.278 --> 00:05:38.588
a high school of 254 kids, I
know it's 254 in our class now.

00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:40.504
I don't know how many of
you follow me on Instagram

00:05:40.504 --> 00:05:42.602
but the happiest day I've
had in the last month

00:05:42.602 --> 00:05:45.742
was when I posted my
report card a month ago

00:05:45.742 --> 00:05:49.470
because I was ranked 243 out
of 254 in my high school.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.247
(audience laughs)

00:05:52.247 --> 00:05:55.069
But there's not a lot of people,
because I was very insular,

00:05:55.069 --> 00:05:57.498
what she can tell you is
that for the first three

00:05:57.498 --> 00:06:00.457
and seven eighths year
of my high school career,

00:06:00.457 --> 00:06:02.629
because I worked in my dad's liquor store

00:06:02.629 --> 00:06:04.130
and because I worked after school,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.558
I didn't do after school sports,

00:06:05.558 --> 00:06:08.894
I didn't hang out, I didn't
have much of a social life,

00:06:08.894 --> 00:06:11.558
I sold baseball cards in
the malls of New Jersey

00:06:11.558 --> 00:06:14.833
and I worked at my dad's
store, I was in my own shit.

00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:17.958
And so, for me, it's fun
to see her right here

00:06:17.958 --> 00:06:21.934
because you could say,
you know, I have empathy.

00:06:21.934 --> 00:06:24.553
One could say, well, you're
happy 'cause you've made it.

00:06:24.553 --> 00:06:27.247
I was fucking super happy
when I wasn't making it

00:06:27.247 --> 00:06:30.430
and doing the shit that
nobody wanted to do.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:32.353
You know, a lot of
people are very confused

00:06:32.353 --> 00:06:33.941
with my narrative, when
people want to troll me,

00:06:33.941 --> 00:06:35.760
they're like, easy for
you, you were handed

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:38.454
three million dollars or
this, that, the other thing.

00:06:38.454 --> 00:06:43.207
I spent the first 12 years
of my career, 15 hours a day,

00:06:43.207 --> 00:06:46.205
paying myself less than
$100,000 a year, every year,

00:06:46.205 --> 00:06:49.200
building my family liquor
store business for my parents.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:53.589
I left at 34 years old with
zero equity in Wine Library,

00:06:53.589 --> 00:06:57.378
no money to my name,
and no equity on paper.

00:06:57.378 --> 00:07:01.092
I basically started over seven years ago.

00:07:01.092 --> 00:07:04.509
But I checked, thanks Dad, but I checked--

00:07:04.509 --> 00:07:06.094
(audience laughs)

00:07:06.094 --> 00:07:08.073
But I checked a very important box for me.

00:07:08.073 --> 00:07:10.374
I don't know how many
of you are immigrants,

00:07:10.374 --> 00:07:12.985
or the children of immigrants,
or how many of you,

00:07:12.985 --> 00:07:17.391
in the same way that I am
(audience cheers)

00:07:17.391 --> 00:07:19.710
that have the great, great
fortune to have their parents

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:23.687
be their heroes, but there's
no greater feeling in life

00:07:23.687 --> 00:07:26.689
than feeling that you've settled
the score with your parents

00:07:26.689 --> 00:07:28.936
and gave them as much as they gave you.

00:07:28.936 --> 00:07:31.264
And that feels incredible
for me of what I did

00:07:31.264 --> 00:07:32.970
in the first part of my
career but here's what I will

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.936
tell you on my mom's birthday.

00:07:34.936 --> 00:07:38.086
My mom did some crazy ass parenting on me.

00:07:38.086 --> 00:07:42.456
First of all, the number one
thing that my mom did for me

00:07:42.456 --> 00:07:45.971
and the number one issue for
the far majority of this room,

00:07:45.971 --> 00:07:48.266
and I'm just lucky of
good parenting and DNA,

00:07:48.266 --> 00:07:50.823
I don't think I'm better than
you, I'm just telling you

00:07:50.823 --> 00:07:53.269
because I see it every day
and because I've read a lot

00:07:53.269 --> 00:07:56.069
of tweets in the last 24
hours of people that are

00:07:56.069 --> 00:07:58.861
in this audience, and I
look at not just the tweet

00:07:58.861 --> 00:08:00.686
that you said you're excited
to see me, I look at all

00:08:00.686 --> 00:08:02.265
the shit you post 'cause
I'm trying to get a sense

00:08:02.265 --> 00:08:05.261
of you fuckers.
(audience laughs)

00:08:05.261 --> 00:08:10.261
The number one gift my mom gave
me is a lack of entitlement.

00:08:10.745 --> 00:08:15.655
If you want to talk--
(audience applauds)

00:08:15.655 --> 00:08:18.389
For all the good shit
that's going on with me,

00:08:18.389 --> 00:08:20.900
I literally think I mean nothing.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.424
I'm being dead serious with you.

00:08:22.424 --> 00:08:25.186
I think I'm the greatest of all time,

00:08:25.186 --> 00:08:27.775
with my confidence, bravado,
and ego, and excitement,

00:08:27.775 --> 00:08:30.321
and the things I see are
happening, but I equally know

00:08:30.321 --> 00:08:33.240
that if I disappeared tomorrow, cool,

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:36.207
I'd have a good social media day and then,

00:08:36.207 --> 00:08:37.566
everybody would move, I'm telling you,

00:08:37.566 --> 00:08:40.551
when Prince and David Bowie
died in that little window,

00:08:40.551 --> 00:08:44.695
and I watched those icons of
culture get about 24 hours

00:08:44.695 --> 00:08:48.603
of love and then everybody
forgot, I was like, fuck.

00:08:48.603 --> 00:08:50.550
Right, I mean it.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.963
I mean it, and so,
what's really empowering

00:08:53.963 --> 00:08:57.382
and what's holding so many
people back here is entitlement.

00:08:57.382 --> 00:08:59.239
You feel like somebody owes
you something because something

00:08:59.239 --> 00:09:03.578
went wrong somewhere
along the way and I don't.

00:09:03.578 --> 00:09:05.697
I actually thing whatever
went wrong for you

00:09:05.697 --> 00:09:07.680
is the best shit that ever happened to you

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:11.740
because adversity is absolutely
the foundation of success.

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:15.394
(audience applauds)

00:09:15.394 --> 00:09:18.247
The other thing I know is I have bad news,

00:09:18.247 --> 00:09:20.708
nobody gives a fuck about your problems.

00:09:20.708 --> 00:09:21.794
(audience laughs)

00:09:21.794 --> 00:09:23.513
And this is super important to understand,

00:09:23.513 --> 00:09:25.772
the second you actually
understand that nobody actually

00:09:25.772 --> 00:09:28.753
gives a shit, the only people
that listen to you complaining

00:09:28.753 --> 00:09:30.919
are your loser fucking friends.

00:09:30.919 --> 00:09:32.572
(audience laughs)

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:35.582
If you like complaining,
just take a step back,

00:09:35.582 --> 00:09:37.937
look around the next time
you're complaining about

00:09:37.937 --> 00:09:41.187
the government, or racial, or feminine.

00:09:43.496 --> 00:09:47.233
I had a guy sit in front of
me the other day crying that

00:09:47.233 --> 00:09:50.252
his life was bad 'cause
he was a white male.

00:09:50.252 --> 00:09:52.010
(audience murmurs)

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:53.867
Rich, white male.

00:09:53.867 --> 00:09:56.348
I was like, oh shit,
we've gone everywhere now.

00:09:56.348 --> 00:09:58.229
(audience laughs)

00:09:58.229 --> 00:10:00.981
You're either on the offense or defense.

00:10:00.981 --> 00:10:02.553
I just want you to know
something, one of the things

00:10:02.553 --> 00:10:04.885
that's been really on my mind
is I think a lot of people

00:10:04.885 --> 00:10:07.508
think they're in the middle,
they're like at trans.

00:10:07.508 --> 00:10:10.529
You're only on the offense or the defense.

00:10:10.529 --> 00:10:13.284
And I've realized that
I've been on the offense

00:10:13.284 --> 00:10:18.165
my whole life because I never
felt entitled to anything.

00:10:18.165 --> 00:10:19.923
An incredible thing
happens when you're born

00:10:19.923 --> 00:10:21.223
with very little.

00:10:21.223 --> 00:10:23.147
I was born in the Soviet
Union, we come to America,

00:10:23.147 --> 00:10:25.885
I live in a studio apartment in Queens.

00:10:25.885 --> 00:10:28.867
My parents bought me six
toys in my entire life

00:10:28.867 --> 00:10:32.233
kind of thing, we took
one, one family vacation

00:10:32.233 --> 00:10:34.827
in my entire life, right.

00:10:34.827 --> 00:10:36.956
When I wanted a Nintendo, my
mom looked me dead in the face

00:10:36.956 --> 00:10:39.369
and said, good, go buy it.

00:10:39.369 --> 00:10:43.477
And that was huge, on the
flip side, what she also did

00:10:43.477 --> 00:10:44.977
was she instilled...

00:10:46.281 --> 00:10:50.201
Let me rephrase, she reinforced
things that came natural

00:10:50.201 --> 00:10:53.024
to me and made them the
most important things.

00:10:53.024 --> 00:10:55.355
While I was getting D's
and F's and every other

00:10:55.355 --> 00:10:58.530
immigrant parent was making
their kids get A's and B's

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:00.381
'cause they thought that's what mattered,

00:11:00.381 --> 00:11:04.243
my mom was accelerating
and going out of her mind

00:11:04.243 --> 00:11:06.713
any time that I showed compassion,

00:11:06.713 --> 00:11:08.743
any time that I showed empathy,

00:11:08.743 --> 00:11:10.581
any time that I showed gratitude.

00:11:10.581 --> 00:11:14.674
I once opened a door for a
woman, this is an amazingly

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.384
interesting story to me.

00:11:16.384 --> 00:11:18.900
I remember after losing a
little league baseball game,

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.325
which I'm extremely competitive,
so basically, if I lost

00:11:22.325 --> 00:11:26.266
at anything, from the ages of five to 13,

00:11:26.266 --> 00:11:27.933
I cried on the spot.

00:11:29.284 --> 00:11:32.867
Like some straight
fucking real tears shit.

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:36.163
So we had a devastating
loss in the little league

00:11:36.163 --> 00:11:39.040
baseball game that we lost,
I mean, I remember we lost

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.875
to the cougars, three
to two, those fucking--

00:11:41.875 --> 00:11:44.329
(hisses)
Anyway.

00:11:44.329 --> 00:11:46.796
We went to McDonald's 'cause,
my mom didn't like buying shit

00:11:46.796 --> 00:11:48.533
but she knew I was super sad,
so she was going to buy me

00:11:48.533 --> 00:11:51.731
a fucking Big Mac, so
we went to McDonald's.

00:11:51.731 --> 00:11:53.586
We got there and I will
remember this for the rest of

00:11:53.586 --> 00:11:58.163
my life, I open, I was eight,
I opened a door for a woman.

00:11:58.163 --> 00:12:01.884
You know, just walking, just
opened the door for a woman.

00:12:01.884 --> 00:12:06.217
You would've thought that
I won a Nobel Peace Prize.

00:12:07.135 --> 00:12:10.061
My mom made such a big deal about that.

00:12:10.061 --> 00:12:14.283
You know, there's a
legendary story for my mom

00:12:14.283 --> 00:12:16.758
when, on my 10th birthday,
we went to Toys "R" Us

00:12:16.758 --> 00:12:19.717
and because Cabbage Patch
Kids were so hard to get

00:12:19.717 --> 00:12:23.362
they had them, so I took all
my money and bought my sister

00:12:23.362 --> 00:12:25.373
the Cabbage Patch on my birthday.

00:12:25.373 --> 00:12:28.325
And that was a good one,
that's better than the door,

00:12:28.325 --> 00:12:30.498
but still, you have to
understand what she did,

00:12:30.498 --> 00:12:33.443
and honestly, for all
of you that watch me,

00:12:33.443 --> 00:12:36.733
it's what I'm doing for my
community with my content.

00:12:36.733 --> 00:12:40.182
We, my friends, more than ever now,

00:12:40.182 --> 00:12:42.682
we need to focus on the truth.

00:12:44.038 --> 00:12:45.455
Here's the truth,

00:12:46.928 --> 00:12:50.347
